WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.040
We have science to thank for&nbsp;
breakthroughs in medicine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.360
improving people’s lives, and even&nbsp;
unlocking some mysteries of the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.920
But can science tell us which movie will make us&nbsp;
shed the most tears? Stay tuned as we find out.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.080
Very few people might like sadness as&nbsp;
a concept, but there's no denying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.600
there's a time and place for sitting&nbsp;
down to watch a sad movie. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.480
exactly what constitutes a sad movie and how&nbsp;
it can be experienced is a more difficult thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.480
to figure out. We all have those certain&nbsp;
films that just tug on our heartstrings.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.040
Some prime examples include the horrifically&nbsp;
depressing ending in Million Dollar Baby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.600
the haunting journey portrayed in The Pianist,&nbsp;
and the gut-wrenching conclusion to The Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.160
Mile. Each movie works in different ways to&nbsp;
make the audience weep for the characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.800
and all are critically acclaimed for&nbsp;
their success in evoking such sadness.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.520
Thanks to the large variety of things people&nbsp;
convey as sad and the vast array of films that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.800
depict said things, you could say the list&nbsp;
of sad movies is nearly endless. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.000
if you were to ask someone what&nbsp;
the saddest movie of all time is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.520
they would probably only be able to&nbsp;
give you a subjective answer — that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.240
unless you ask the right people. See, while it's&nbsp;
easy to assume that it's pretty much impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.720
to designate a single film as the saddest&nbsp;
movie of all time, Smithsonian Magazine's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.760
Richard Chin thinks otherwise. He spotlights&nbsp;
an astounding scientific study by psychology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.800
researchers Robert Levenson and James Gross, who&nbsp;
have apparently succeeded in such an endeavor.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.160
Working at the University of California, Berkeley&nbsp;
back in 1988, Levenson and Gross were researching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.240
the relationship between movie scenes and&nbsp;
emotion. Specifically, they were searching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:39.200
for a scene that succeeded in eliciting&nbsp;
only a single emotion at a time, Smithsonian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.000
Magazine reports. On the sadness front, the&nbsp;
winner was as clear as it was surprising.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.880
After testing 78 carefully vetted sad movie&nbsp;
scenes across almost 500 undergraduate volunteers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.560
they discovered that the ending scene in Franco&nbsp;
Zeffirelli's 1979 sports drama The Champ was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.240
distilled sadness, evoking the emotion more often&nbsp;
than any other scene. To put this in context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.640
second place went to the famous scene in Bambi&nbsp;
where the titular character's mother dies.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.600
Since the results of the research were published&nbsp;
in 1995, The Champ has been used in a great many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.440
psychology experiments and features in over 300&nbsp;
papers. This is largely due to the scene's ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.520
to cause sadness in test subjects in a powerful,&nbsp;
yet comparatively ethical way. Levenson said:

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.600
"It's wonderful for our purposes.&nbsp;
The theme of irrevocable loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.560
it's all compressed into&nbsp;
that two or three minutes."

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.040
Curiously enough, despite its off-the-charts&nbsp;
emotional punch, critics don't consider The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.720
Champ a particularly great movie. It only has a&nbsp;
36 percent Tomatometer score on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.760
so while scientists may be&nbsp;
convinced of its importance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.360
perhaps being a sad movie doesn't&nbsp;
make it a good movie. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.880
its audience score is a respectable 82 percent,&nbsp;
so maybe critics just don't like feeling sad.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.640
So what makes The Champ such a sad movie?&nbsp;
Well, if you know what it’s about, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.640
easy to understand why the film is custom-made&nbsp;
to put your feelings through the wringer. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:05.520
movie follows Billy "Champ" Flynn, played by&nbsp;
Jon Voight — an aging boxer who's struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.560
to be a good father for his adorable son, T.J.,&nbsp;
played by Ricky Schroder. As this is happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.920
the boy's estranged mother, played by Fay Dunaway,&nbsp;
attempts to pry the kid away from his dad.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.520
All three proceed to go through the wringer&nbsp;
throughout the movie's duration. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.200
scientifically ultra-sad final scene, Billy dies&nbsp;
in the aftermath of his desperate comeback match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.840
in plain view of the distraught T.J., who&nbsp;
slowly realizes that his father is dead.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:36.853
"T.J. please."
"I want champ!”&nbsp;

00:03:36.853 --> 00:03:36.880
“T.J.”
“I want champ!"&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.520
“TJ. Please, T.J. Listen to me. He's gone."

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.840
The heart-rending, nearly three-minute&nbsp;
scene consists pretty much entirely of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.200
T.J. uncontrollably crying over his&nbsp;
father's earthly remains. Pretty sad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.200
right? What's more, the movie has plenty&nbsp;
of star power in Voight and Dunaway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.880
and young Schroder actually won a Golden Globe&nbsp;
for his role as the emotive T.J. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.840
you can be sure that all those sad scenes and&nbsp;
dramatic beats get driven home with five-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.720
ability. The end result is so extremely sad&nbsp;
that, according to Smithsonian Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.160
even the scientists in charge of the experiment&nbsp;
weren't immune to its power. James Gross admitted:

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.760
"I still feel sad when I see&nbsp;
that boy crying his heart out."

